        - [APPLIED 2026-04-29] Fix Loop C t-test claim
            - chapter.log lines 36-37 and chapter.md lines 43-44 say "1/1: t-test breaks (zero variance, NaN)" and "0/10 and 10/10: ... t-test breaks (zero variance again)." Only the 1/1 case returns NaN. The 0/N and N/N cases return t = ±inf, p = 0.0. That is "produces a number, but the number is not informative", not "breaks". Restate: "1/1: t-test returns NaN, the only test that fails to produce a number. 0/N and N/N: t-test returns p = 0 with an infinite t-statistic; that is technically a number but it claims more certainty than the data carry, the same direction of error as normal-approx but more extreme."
        - [APPLIED 2026-04-29] Reframe the Fisher-exact row honestly
            - chapter.log line 8 and chapter.md line 9 describe Fisher as "Treat our 2x2 (observed vs an 'ideal fair' reference of equal size) as a contingency table." This is non-standard. Fisher exact is for two sampled binomials, not for one sample versus an idealized reference. Replace the lead-in with: "Fisher exact test, force-fitted to a one-sample problem. We pair our observed (k, n-k) with an idealized reference of equal size (n/2, n/2) and run Fisher on the 2x2. This is the wrong tool, on purpose, to see what happens when a two-sample test is applied to a one-sample question."
            - chapter.log line 16 and chapter.md line 19 say Fisher gives a larger p-value "because the reference 50/50 has variance too." Replace with: "Fisher gives a larger p-value because it is paying for variance the reference does not actually have. As we grow the reference toward infinity (treating 50/50 as known), Fisher converges to the exact binomial p-value. With reference size n, Fisher is always more conservative than exact binomial and the gap shrinks with reference size."
            - Add a brief side computation in generate.py or notebook.ipynb: at 60/100, run Fisher with reference sizes n, 10n, 100n, 10000n; print Fisher converging from 0.20 toward 0.057. This makes the point concrete.
        - [APPLIED 2026-04-29] Make the chi-square / z equivalence explicit
            - The notebook output already shows z and chi2 are bitwise identical at every printed p-value because chi^2 = z^2 for a 2-cell goodness-of-fit with no continuity correction. Add to Loop A in chapter.log and chapter.md, after the "Observe" block: "Note: chi-square goodness-of-fit on (k, n-k) versus (n/2, n/2) and the normal-approx z-test give identical p-values at every row. They are algebraically the same test, since the chi-square statistic equals z squared and the chi-square reference distribution with one degree of freedom equals the squared standard normal. We list them separately because they generalize differently: chi-square extends to more than two categories, z extends to other shapes of null."
        - [APPLIED 2026-04-29] Fix the one-sample t on 0/1 framing
            - chapter.log line 9 and chapter.md line 10 dismiss the t-test with "Treats each toss as a continuous draw. Wrong-shape model on purpose; useful for comparison." Add an explanation of why it nonetheless tracks the z-test closely on 0/1 data: "On 0/1 data the sample variance is hat-p (1 minus hat-p) times n/(n-1), so the one-sample t is a Wald-style proportion test with the SE plugged in from the data instead of the null. The t reference distribution is the wrong shape, the data are not normal, but for moderate n away from the boundary the answer lands very close to the z-test. The one-sample t fails on the same edge cases (0/N, N/N, 1/1) as the z-test, and worse: it returns infinite t at 0/N and N/N, and NaN at 1/1."
